/**
	Load and play a sound in one go, in a way that cleans it up automatically when it has finished.
	sound@ sound_play(const string&in path, const vector&in position = vector(FLOAT_MAX, FLOAT_MAX, FLOAT_MAX), float volume = FLOAT_MAX, float pan = FLOAT_MAX, float pitch = FLOAT_MAX, mixer@ mix = null, const pack_interface@ pack_file = sound_default_pack, bool autoplay = true);
	## Arguments:
		* const string&in path: the file to load the sound from.
		* const vector&in position = vector(FLOAT_MAX, FLOAT_MAX, FLOAT_MAX): where to play the sound in 3d space. The default of FLOAT_MAX for every axis means the sound is not positioned.
		* float volume = FLOAT_MAX: the volume of the sound, see `mixer::volume`. The default leaves it at full volume.
		* float pan = FLOAT_MAX: the pan of the sound. The default leaves it centered.
		* float pitch = FLOAT_MAX: the pitch of the sound. The default leaves it at its normal pitch.
		* mixer@ mix = null: the mixer to play the sound through, such as the mixer of the object making the sound.
		* const pack_interface@ pack_file = sound_default_pack: the pack to load the sound from.
		* bool autoplay = true: whether to start playing straight away.
	## Returns:
		sound@: the sound, or null if it could not be loaded.
	## Remarks:
		This is the same as `audio_engine::play()` on the default engine. The sound has `autoclose` turned on, so it plays to its end and then frees itself even if you don't keep the handle, which makes this the easiest way to play one off sound effects. Keep the handle if you need to stop or move the sound.
		Passing the mixer of the object making the sound positions it with the object, see the remarks of the mixer class.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	sound_play("C:/Windows/media/ding.wav", vector(5, 0, 0));
	wait(1000);
}
